course: dictionary
lesson: 5845
title: One word, three meanings: Malay
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words wear different coats. «Malay» can name a people, a language, or be a describing word. Context tells you which one. Ask yourself: is the sentence talking about a group of people, a way of speaking, or a thing that belongs to them?
explain: «Orang-utan means person of the forest in Malay.» Here Malay is the language. You can tell because the sentence says the word means something in a language. «Malacca on the Malay Peninsula grew into one of Asia's greatest ports» is different: Malay is a place name, so it tells you where Malacca is.
explain: When Malay describes something, it works like a colour word. «Malay food» means food linked to the Malay people and their culture, like nasi lemak. Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If «food linked to the Malay people» still fits, you have it right.
fact: Malay is a word for a people, a language, and a describing word.
fact: The Malays live mostly in Malaysia, Indonesia, Brunei, Singapore and southern Thailand.
fact: Malay is an official language of Malaysia, Brunei and Singapore, and Indonesian is a form of it.
fact: As an adjective, «Malay» describes things to do with the Malay people, their language or their culture.
exercise: In «Orang-utan means person of the forest in Malay», what does Malay name? | A kind of forest | *A language | A country made of islands | A type of boat
exercise: In «Malay food», what does Malay tell you? | *Food linked to the Malay people and their culture | Food from a ship at sea | The name of a shop | Food that is always cold
exercise: Where do most Malay people live? | Mostly in Canada | Only in Scotland | *Malaysia, Indonesia, Brunei, Singapore and southern Thailand | Only in Australia
test: Which of these mixes into Singlish along with English? | Welsh | *Malay | Swahili | Dutch
test: «Malay» is an official language of which of these places? | Iceland | Kenya | *Brunei | Peru
exam: Indonesian is a form of which language? | *Malay | Greek | Latin | Tamil
